#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mutation-notify — file the durable notification for a post-merge mutation run that
# produced no usable verdict. Called by .github/workflows/mutation.yml's `notify` job;
# runnable by hand, which is the point (see "Verifying this" below).
#
# usage: scripts/mutation-notify <repo> <sha> <run-id> <run-url> <matrix-result> <event-name> <ref-name>
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AS A SCRIPT AND NOT AS AN INLINE `run:` BLOCK. This code only ever
# executes in the failure case, so a defect in it is invisible until the one moment it
# is needed — which is exactly how #98 stayed open for a month. Inline YAML cannot be
# run against a stubbed `gh`; a script can, and scripts/test-mutation-notify does.
#
# ONE ISSUE PER COMMIT, NOT ONE ISSUE FOREVER. The first version keyed the tracking
# issue on the `mutation-gate` label alone, so every later run commented on whichever
# issue was already open. Three consecutive `main` commits (b62176d0, e1ec3a6e,
# 83afbbfb) therefore landed as three comments on a ten-day-old #181 — which is the
# "grey X nobody opens" failure one level up: the notification exists, is loud, and
# still reads as an old known problem rather than as news about this commit. The title
# now carries the short sha, so a new commit opens a new issue and a re-dispatch of the
# same commit comments on that commit's issue.
#
# WHY THE TITLE IS THE KEY AND NOT A MARKER IN THE BODY. `gh issue list --json title`
# is one call and an exact string compare; a body marker needs a per-issue fetch or a
# `--search` whose matching rules are GitHub's, not ours. The title is also what a
# human scanning the issue list actually reads.
#
# ADVISORY ENRICHMENT ONLY. The per-shard breakdown comes from the Actions API. If that
# call fails the report is still filed, just without the breakdown — fetching detail
# must never be the reason the notification goes missing.
#
# VERIFYING THIS SCRIPT: scripts/test-mutation-notify runs it against a stubbed `gh`
# and asserts both routing branches, the API-failure fallback, and that an apostrophe
# in the shard list does not break the heredocs.
set -euo pipefail

repo="${1:?usage: mutation-notify <repo> <sha> <run-id> <run-url> <matrix-result> <event-name> <ref-name>}"
sha="${2:?missing sha}"
run_id="${3:?missing run id}"
run_url="${4:?missing run url}"
matrix_result="${5:?missing matrix result}"
event_name="${6:?missing event name}"
ref_name="${7:?missing ref name}"

short_sha="${sha:0:12}"
title="Mutation gate failed on ${ref_name} — ${short_sha}"
report="$(mktemp -t mutation-report)"

legs="$(gh api --paginate \
    "repos/$repo/actions/runs/$run_id/jobs?per_page=100" \
    --jq '.jobs[] | select(.name | startswith("Stryker (")) | [.conclusion, .name] | @tsv' \
    2>/dev/null || true)"

legs_md="$(printf '%s\n' "$legs" \
    | awk -F'\t' 'NF==2 && $1!="success" { printf "- `%s` — **%s**\n", $2, $1 }')"

if [ -z "$legs_md" ]; then
    legs_md="- (per-shard conclusions unavailable; matrix result: \`$matrix_result\`)"
fi

# Build the report in a FILE, with the heredocs at statement level and the static half
# quoted (<<'MD'). Not cosmetic: a heredoc nested inside a `$( )` command substitution
# still has its body scanned for quotes on bash 3.2, so a single apostrophe ("the
# shard's log") turns the whole script into a syntax error — and this code only ever
# runs in the failure case, where that would silence the notification exactly like the
# bug it was written for. `--body-file` also means backticks need no escaping, so the
# Markdown below reads as Markdown.
{
    cat <<EOF
The mutation run did not succeed for \`$sha\` on \`$ref_name\` (matrix result:
\`$matrix_result\`, trigger: \`$event_name\`).

Run: $run_url

Shards that did not succeed:

$legs_md
EOF
    cat <<'MD'

Mutation runs post-merge by design, so this did not block the merge — it
needs someone to act. There are three shapes, and the right action differs
per shape; each shard's conclusion above says which one you are looking at.

- **`failure` — mutation score below threshold.** The adversarial suite
  stopped biting. The fix is to add or strengthen a test, not to revert.
- **`failure` — a vacuous run, or one that measured the wrong thing.**
  The gate is broken, not the suite (stryker-net#3094); the shard's log
  carries an `::error::` annotation naming it, emitted by
  `scripts/assert-mutation-run`. More urgent than a low score: it means
  the previous greens were meaningless.
- **`cancelled` / `timed_out` — no verdict produced.** The shard hit
  `timeout-minutes`, so there is no score at all, and nothing here says
  the suite got worse. What needs looking at is the run's cost and its
  budget — not the tests.

Each shard uploads its Stryker log as a **`stryker-log-<shard>`** artifact
on the run linked above; that is how to tell a vacuous run from a
below-threshold score. One gap worth knowing rather than discovering: a
shard killed by `timeout-minutes` is *cancelled*, and a cancelled job never
reaches its upload step — so the third case above is precisely the one with
no artifact. It is a budget question anyway, not a log-reading one.

This issue is keyed on the commit above. A later commit opens its own issue
rather than commenting here, so a stale one closing does not lose news about
a newer one.
MD
} > "$report"

# The label may not exist yet; creating it must not fail the job.
gh label create mutation-gate \
    --repo "$repo" \
    --description "Post-merge mutation run needs attention" \
    --color B60205 2>/dev/null || true

# Exact-title match among the OPEN mutation-gate issues. `--limit 100` is generous
# enough that a plausible backlog cannot push this commit's own issue off the page,
# and the compare is exact so a similar title from another commit cannot absorb it.
#
# The title reaches jq through the ENVIRONMENT, not through string interpolation into
# the jq program. A ref name is attacker-influenced (anyone who can open a PR can name
# a branch), and a `"` or `\` in it would either break the program or let it be
# rewritten — a shape that would only ever be exercised in the failure path.
existing="$(MUTATION_ISSUE_TITLE="$title" gh issue list --repo "$repo" --state open \
    --label mutation-gate --limit 100 --json number,title \
    --jq 'map(select(.title == env.MUTATION_ISSUE_TITLE)) | .[0].number // empty' 2>/dev/null || true)"

if [ -n "$existing" ]; then
    gh issue comment "$existing" --repo "$repo" --body-file "$report"
else
    gh issue create --repo "$repo" \
        --title "$title" \
        --label mutation-gate \
        --body-file "$report"
fi
